tructured">Jonsen AR, Siegler M, Winslade W. Clinical ethics: a practical approach to ethical decisions in clinical medicine. 6th ed. New York: McGraw-Hill; 2006. </citation></ref><ref id="CR10"><label>10.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Raymont</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Bingley</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Buchanan</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>David</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Hayward</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Wessely</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Prevalence of mental incapacity in medical inpatients and associated risk factors: cross-sectional study</article-title><source>Lancet.</source><year>2004</year><volume>364</volume><issue>9443</issue><fpage>1421</fpage><lpage>1427</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0140-6736(04)17224-3</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Raymont V, Bingley W, Buchanan A, David AS, Hayward P, Wessely S, et al. Prevalence of mental incapacity in medical inpatients and associated risk factors: cross-sectional study. Lancet. 2004;364(9443):1421&#x02013;7, Oct 16&#x02013;22. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15488217</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR11"><label>11.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Karlawish</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Casarett</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name><name><surname>James</surname><given-names>BD</given-names></name><name><surname>Xie</surname><given-names>SX</given-names></name><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>SY</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The ability of persons with Alzheimer disease (AD) to make a decision about taking an AD treatment</article-title><source>Neurology</source><year>2005</year><volume>64</volume><issue>9</issue><fpage>1514</fpage><lpage>1519</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1212/01.WNL.0000160000.01742.9D</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Karlawish JH, Casarett DJ, James BD, Xie SX, Kim SY. The ability of persons with Alzheimer disease (AD) to make a decision about taking an AD treatment. Neurology. 2005;64(9):1514&#x02013;9, May 10. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15883310</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR12"><label>12.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mattimore</surname><given-names>TJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Wenger</surname><given-names>NS</given-names></name><name><surname>Desbiens</surname><given-names>NA</given-names></name><name><surname>Teno</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Hamel</surname><given-names>MB</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Surrogate and physician understanding of patients&#x02019; preferences for living permanently in a nursing home</article-title><source>J Am Geriatr Soc</source><year>1997</year><volume>45</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>818</fpage><lpage>824</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Mattimore TJ, Wenger NS, Desbiens NA, Teno JM, Hamel MB, Liu H, et al. Surrogate and physician understanding of patients&#x02019; preferences for living permanently in a nursing home. J Am Geriatr Soc. 1997;45(7):818&#x02013;24, Jul. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9215332</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR13"><label>13.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Karlawish</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Quill</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Meier</surname><given-names>DE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A consensus-based approach to providing palliative care to patients who lack decision-making capacity. ACP-ASIM End-of-Life Care Consensus Panel. American College of Physicians-American Society of Internal Medicine</article-title><source>Ann Intern Med</source><year>1999</year><volume>130</volume><issue>10</issue><fpage>835</fpage><lpage>840</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Karlawish JH, Quill T, Meier DE. A consensus-based approach to providing palliative care to patients who lack decision-making capacity. ACP-ASIM End-of-Life Care Consensus Panel. American College of Physicians-American Society of Internal Medicine. Ann Intern Med. 1999;130(10):835&#x02013;40, May 18. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10366374</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>